                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'In a cooler climate, a small leak might sit overnight without much harm. In Rocky Mount, especially from May through September, the math is different. Warm air holds a lot of moisture, so as water evaporates from wet floors it drives indoor humidity up fast. That damp air then soaks into everything else in the house: cabinets swell, paint blisters, closets start to smell and mold finds new places to grow, even in rooms the water never touched.',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'The structure of local homes adds to the urgency. Water on the floor of a crawl space home does not stay on the floor; it seeps into the subfloor and drips onto the insulation and soil below. Water in a slab home spreads under flooring and into the bottom of drywall. Either way, the longer it sits, the more has to be cut out.',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'Getting liquid water out quickly is the single biggest factor in how much of your home can be saved. It is faster and simpler to extract water than to dry it out of the air later.',
