                [
                    'type' => 'h2',
                    'text' => 'Slab Homes vs. Crawl Space Homes: Where Water Goes in Rocky Mount',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'Rocky Mount has two main kinds of homes, and water behaves differently in each.',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'In slab homes, which are more common in newer neighborhoods, water stays on top of the concrete. It soaks carpet and pad, slips under vinyl plank and laminate, wicks up drywall and baseboards, and swells the bottoms of cabinets. Extraction focuses on floors and walls, and we check under flooring and behind cabinets for trapped water.',
                ],
                [
                    'type' => 'p',
                    'text' => 'In crawl space homes, which make up much of Rocky Mount and the surrounding countryside, water drops through gaps around pipes, the edges of flooring and the subfloor itself. Some stays in the floor system, and the rest lands on the vapor barrier and soil below. A room can look nearly dry while the subfloor and insulation underneath are soaked. That is why we always check below the floor, and often extract and dry from both sides at once.',
